s://api.github.com/users/JoostK/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/JoostK/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2019-05-01T21:54:13Z","updated_at":"2019-05-01T21:54:13Z","body":"@spock123 thanks for investigating and sharing the code snippet!\r\n\r\nBy the looks of it, this problem is caused because of statically analyzing `NgModule` metadata to list all lazy routes in the program, a process that the CLI runs to support the `loadChildren` pattern for lazy modules.\r\n\r\nWhen compiling Ivy code, the compiler itself does not attempt to statically analyze `NgModule.providers` as there is no need for it to interpret that code, it it just compiled verbatim into the correct location. The lazy listing of routes *is* however still implemented in the Ivy compiler so we should probably fix it to deal with this scenario.\r\n\r\n@alan-agius4 is it possible to disable the listing of lazy routes? I found some code paths in the CLI that disable it based on a compiler option, but that option doesn't seem to be exposed to users.","author_association":"MEMBER","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/angular/angular/issues/comments/488443867/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"JoostK","id":123679,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEyMzY3OQ==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com